            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Russian for to go hoarse, then the Russian for to tickle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: сыпь",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "сыпь (syp) — \"a rash\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "После клубники у ребёнка появилась сыпь. Pósle klubníki u rebyónka poyavílas syp. After the strawberries the child came out in a rash."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "сыпь (syp) is built on сыпать 'to pour, to strew', which is inherited from Proto-Slavic sypati (EtymDB): a rash is strewn over the skin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
